cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this ownership decomposition, its matched-vintage audit, its within-panel stability audit, or the public artifact, cite the manuscript and this v0.1.1 archival release. Corresponding author: Jingzhe Peng, pengjingzhe_quiet@foxmail.com. The v0.1.1 git tag is the archival release; no DOI has been minted yet. The source code is MIT-licensed, code only; the ICEWS-derived aggregate results are not MIT."
title: "Actor-side components of dyadic event forecasts: an ownership decomposition, specification dependence, and a matched-vintage audit in a 196-actor ICEWS panel"
abstract: >-
  Public artifact and L0-L2 verification release for the V2 manuscript on
  actor-side components of dyadic event forecasts in a 196-actor ICEWS panel,
  1995-2023. The release ships code, tests, the V1 confirmatory and stability
  aggregates, and the V2 matched-vintage aggregate tree. The analysis applies a
  post-hoc six-class ownership convention to a plan-declared feature list and
  fixes unit and sign, so every logistic forecast carries an exact
  group-additive decomposition into source-role, target-role, dyad and
  non-actor groups. The ownership convention has no pre-run record. It is a
  design convention and audit, not a new estimator: the decomposition is the
  logistic identity and coincides with linear SHAP under a training-mean
  background. The class shares on the V2 training window are 45.2% actor-owned
  and 54.8% not actor-owned; only 4 of 49 features are role-specific. The V1
  49-feature model loses to a 2017-vintage warm baseline on the one-shot
  unit-fresh 20% dyad holdout (0.217875 vs 0.209729 log-loss). A post-hoc
  matched-vintage audit on the same burned holdout finds that the 47-feature
  model beats a 2014-vintage warm baseline by 0.020954 (95% CI
  0.015443-0.026465), a 25-feature model with no actor-owned column beats it by
  0.017743, and the 22 added columns contribute 0.003211. The as-specified 49
  is null against the matched comparator under the H0 cache. The V2 analysis is
  post hoc and secondary; the V1 49-feature result remains the plan-declared
  primary. The release is one paper path, not a reusable framework, and is
  executable at L0-L2 only (read the frozen aggregates, verify their hashes and
  contracts, re-render the notebooks). The restricted 50-refit audit code and
  all ICEWS inputs are not redistributed; row-level predictions, fitted
  coefficients and labels are withheld. The confirmatory evaluation is
  retrospective, not time-fresh. The stability audit is provisional,
  holdout-row-excluded and based on 50 dyad-cluster bootstrap refits. The
  public experiment scripts are a five-seed preview of the audit method and do
  not regenerate the 50-refit holdout-row-excluded aggregates. No general claim
  is made about graph features, persistence or any corpus beyond this panel.
  Source code is MIT-licensed; ICEWS-derived aggregate results are not MIT and
  remain under the ICEWS research-and-teaching terms. This is the v0.1.1
  archival release; no DOI has been minted.
authors:
